// A `::<…>` type argument list with **no call to instantiate** is rejected on its own terms.
//
// The message this replaces was chumsky's expected-set — `` found `(` expected `.` `` — which named
// a token the writer never typed on purpose and said nothing about the rule. It was also what the
// composed spelling `Repo::<Todo>.blank::<int>()` used to produce before it parsed at all, so the
// one report covered both a real gap in the language and a genuine mistake, and read as neither.
//
// The rule it now states is the true one: a type argument list is part of a CALL, never an
// expression of its own. Every legal position is in the help — `f::<T>(args)`,
// `recv.method::<T>(args)`, `Type::<T>.method(args)`, and the two together.
//
// The span is the token that followed, which is exactly where the missing call should have begun.
// expect: error E0003 at 27:23
// expect: exit 1
class Box<T> {
    value: T

    fn new(v: T): Box<T> {
        return Box { value: v };
    }

    fn choose<U>(other: U): U {
        return other;
    }
}
b = Box.new(1);
x = b.choose::<string>;
echo x;
